import type { SpeakingInterviewPack } from './speaking-model.js';
import { additionalSpeakingPart3Packs } from './speaking-part3-extra.js';

const bands = [3, 3.5, 4, 4.5, 5, 5.5, 6, 6.5, 7, 7.5, 8];
const discussion = (topic: string, pairs: [string, string][]): SpeakingInterviewPack => ({ topic, band: bands[topic.length % bands.length]!, questions: pairs.map(([prompt, answer]) => ({ prompt, answer })) });
const initialPacks: SpeakingInterviewPack[] = [
  discussion('Homework and independent learning', [
    ['Why do schools set work to be completed at home?', 'I think the main purpose is to let pupils practise without immediate help from a teacher. A short mathematics exercise can reveal whether someone really understands the method. However, that benefit disappears if parents complete the work, or if the task is so long that children simply copy answers.'],
    ['How should homework change as children get older?', 'Younger children need brief, clear activities that they can finish successfully, perhaps reading aloud for a few minutes. Older students can manage longer projects and choose how to organise them. The increase should be gradual, because age alone does not mean every learner has developed effective planning skills.'],
    ['Can homework increase differences between pupils?', 'Yes, because homes offer very different conditions. One child may have a quiet desk and an adult who explains difficult words, while another shares a crowded room. Schools should provide study spaces and avoid assuming that every family can buy materials or give expert help.'],
    ['What makes an independent learning task useful?', 'It needs a clear purpose and feedback that helps the learner improve. For example, comparing two news reports teaches source evaluation if the teacher later discusses how evidence was selected. Merely collecting a finished page without responding to the reasoning gives pupils little guidance about their next attempt.'],
    ['Should homework results strongly influence school grades?', 'I would be cautious because the teacher cannot know exactly how much outside help a pupil received. Homework can show effort and inform teaching, but important grades should include supervised work and discussion. That combination is fairer than treating a polished take-home project as completely independent evidence.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Languages and cultural identity', [
    ['Why do some families maintain more than one language?', 'A family language connects children with relatives and stories that may not translate neatly into another language. It can also make visits less awkward for older relatives who speak only that language. Maintaining it requires regular use, though, rather than assuming children will learn simply by hearing occasional words.'],
    ['How can communities protect smaller languages?', 'They need places where the language is useful, such as local media, lessons and public events. A recording archive is valuable, but a language cannot survive only as something stored. Supporting speakers to use it in ordinary relationships matters more than displaying it ceremonially once a year.'],
    ['Does learning an international language weaken local identity?', 'Not necessarily. It can give people another tool without replacing the language they use at home. Problems arise when education or employment treats the local language as inferior. A bilingual approach can expand opportunities while showing that local literature and community knowledge still deserve respect.'],
    ['Why is translation sometimes difficult even when the words are familiar?', 'Words carry cultural assumptions and social relationships as well as dictionary meanings. A polite request in one language may sound abrupt if translated directly. Translators need to consider the audience and purpose, especially in humour or public information, rather than replace each word mechanically.'],
    ['Should children decide which languages they want to continue studying?', 'Their preferences should matter, particularly as they become older, but adults can explain opportunities they may not yet recognise. A child might resist a heritage-language class while still valuing conversations with grandparents. Flexible activities can maintain that connection without turning every family interaction into a lesson.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Changing career later in life', [
    ['Why do people change careers after many years of employment?', 'Sometimes their industry changes, but they may also discover that the work no longer suits their interests or family needs. An office worker might prefer a practical role with visible results. Financial commitments make the decision difficult, so a change often follows considerable preparation rather than sudden dissatisfaction.'],
    ['What advantages can older career changers bring?', 'They often bring transferable skills such as dealing with customers, organising deadlines and resolving disagreements. Those abilities can help even when technical knowledge is new. However, employers should not assume that experience in one occupation automatically provides every skill required in another; training is still important.'],
    ['How can employers support someone entering a new profession?', 'A structured introduction helps the person understand unfamiliar procedures without feeling embarrassed about basic questions. A mentor can explain the practical details that training manuals miss. It is also useful to recognise existing strengths, so the employee is not treated as inexperienced in every aspect of working life.'],
    ['Is a short training course enough for a career change?', 'It depends on the role. A course may introduce digital design tools, but medical or safety-critical work needs extensive supervised training. Even in less regulated jobs, a small real project can show whether classroom knowledge transfers to practice before someone gives up stable employment.'],
    ['Should public funding help adults retrain?', 'Yes, where retraining addresses genuine employment needs and is accessible to people who cannot afford long unpaid study. Evening provision and support with childcare may be as important as course fees. Funding should be checked against useful outcomes, rather than paying for certificates with little value to employers.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Public parks and urban heat', [
    ['Why are shaded public spaces important in cities?', 'They make walking and waiting outdoors more comfortable, particularly during hot weather. A shaded route to a bus stop can be more useful than a large park that residents rarely reach. Designers should think about everyday journeys and vulnerable users, rather than only the appearance of green spaces.'],
    ['What problems can arise when a city plants many new trees?', 'Trees need suitable soil, water and space as they mature. Planting them in tiny pavement openings can leave roots damaged and branches badly positioned. A smaller number of well-maintained trees may provide more lasting benefit than a publicity campaign that plants thousands without funding their care.'],
    ['Should parks prioritise sports facilities or natural areas?', 'I would aim for a balance, because residents use parks for different reasons. A playing field supports organised activity, while quiet planted areas offer shade and habitat. The layout can separate noisy games from relaxation instead of expecting one part of the park to meet every need.'],
    ['How can authorities find out whether a park serves local residents?', 'They should observe use at different times and speak with people who do not currently visit, not just regular users. For example, parents may avoid a park because toilets are unavailable. Visitor totals alone would miss that obstacle and could make an inaccessible park appear successful.'],
    ['Can private gardens replace the need for public green space?', 'No, because many households do not have a garden, and private space does not provide the same community access. Public parks also connect walking routes and allow larger activities. Gardens contribute to greenery, but access to shade and recreation should not depend entirely on housing wealth.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Older adults and digital services', [
    ['Why do some older adults find digital services difficult?', 'The difficulty may come from unfamiliar design rather than age itself. Small text, changing passwords and unexplained error messages can discourage any user. Someone who has never needed an online account may also lack practice, so patient instruction and a predictable interface are more helpful than assumptions about ability.'],
    ['Should essential services keep an offline option?', 'Yes, at least while some people cannot use the digital route reliably. A bank or medical service is different from an optional shopping app, because exclusion can have serious consequences. Telephone or in-person support can remain available while digital access improves, even if it costs more to provide.'],
    ['What is an effective way to teach digital skills?', 'I would start with a useful task chosen by the learner, such as sending a photograph or booking an appointment. Practising that sequence builds confidence and gives the lesson a purpose. Covering many abstract features at once can overload beginners, especially if they have no opportunity to repeat them.'],
    ['How can families help without taking away independence?', 'They can explain each step and let the person operate the device, rather than completing everything quickly on their behalf. Written reminders may help between visits. Security advice is also important, but it should encourage sensible checking rather than make the learner afraid of every online interaction.'],
    ['Will generational differences in digital confidence disappear?', 'They may become smaller, but technology will continue changing. People familiar with today’s applications may struggle with a different system in later life. That means services should always provide understandable guidance and support, rather than assuming that the next generation will never need assistance.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Advertising and consumer choice', [
    ['Why does advertising influence people who know it is persuasive?', 'Awareness does not remove emotional associations or repeated exposure. A product linked with comfort or belonging can become familiar even when the buyer recognises the sales intention. However, influence is not automatic; price, previous experience and recommendations may still outweigh a well-designed advertisement.'],
    ['Are advertisements aimed at children different from those aimed at adults?', 'Children may have less experience separating entertainment from selling, especially when a favourite character promotes a product. Adults can also be influenced, but usually understand the transaction more clearly. Rules for children should therefore consider the presentation and audience, rather than merely whether the factual claims are accurate.'],
    ['How has online advertising changed the buying process?', 'It can reach people at the moment they search for something and lead directly to a purchase. That is convenient, but personalised recommendations can make browsing feel like impartial advice. Clear labels and control over tracking help users understand why a particular product is being shown.'],
    ['Should companies be responsible for the behaviour of paid influencers?', 'They should be responsible for agreed promotional claims and ensure that sponsorship is disclosed. If an influencer says a product has a medical benefit, the company cannot simply treat it as a private opinion. Responsibility needs boundaries, though, because a creator may also publish unrelated personal content.'],
    ['Can advertising help consumers make better decisions?', 'Yes, when it provides useful comparisons, prices and conditions that people can check. An advertisement announcing a cheaper transport pass could genuinely help households. The problem is selective information, so consumers need access to fuller details rather than relying entirely on a brief promotional message.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Volunteer training and responsibility', [
    ['Why do people volunteer even when they receive no salary?', 'Some want to support a cause, while others value relationships or the chance to use their skills. A retired accountant might enjoy helping a small charity manage its records. Motivation can change over time, so organisations should recognise practical limits rather than assume goodwill means unlimited availability.'],
    ['When should volunteers receive formal training?', 'Training is essential when mistakes could harm people or expose sensitive information. Someone supporting vulnerable clients needs clearer preparation than a person setting out chairs at an event. The amount should match the responsibility, because unnecessarily lengthy training can discourage people from offering simple, low-risk help.'],
    ['Can unpaid roles replace professional employees?', 'Occasionally volunteers provide specialist skills, but relying on them for core services can make provision unstable. A charity still needs someone accountable for scheduling and safeguarding. Volunteers should strengthen a service rather than become a way to avoid paying for work that requires continuous professional responsibility.'],
    ['How can organisations keep volunteers involved?', 'Clear tasks, realistic time commitments and feedback make people feel that their contribution matters. A volunteer who waits without instructions may stop attending even if the cause is important. It also helps to offer flexible roles, since work schedules and family responsibilities can change unexpectedly.'],
    ['Should schools require pupils to volunteer?', 'A supported community project can introduce pupils to useful work, but compulsory hours alone may encourage minimum compliance. I would combine a limited shared project with choice about the role and reflection afterwards. That preserves an educational purpose without pretending that mandatory participation is entirely voluntary.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Museums and public access', [
    ['What can museums provide that online collections cannot?', 'They allow people to experience scale, materials and arrangement directly, which a photograph may not capture. A large weaving or an ancient tool can make more sense when seen in context. Online collections are valuable for access, but they usually complement rather than fully reproduce the physical visit.'],
    ['Should museum admission always be free?', 'Free entry removes a financial obstacle, especially for families who are unsure whether children will enjoy a visit. However, museums still need stable funding for staff and conservation. A sensible approach might offer free general admission while charging for selected special exhibitions, provided essential collections remain accessible.'],
    ['How can museums attract visitors who rarely attend?', 'They should find out what makes those visitors feel excluded, such as unfamiliar language, opening times or the belief that the collection has no relevance to them. Community partnerships and clear interpretation can help. Advertising alone will not solve an experience that people expect to be uncomfortable.'],
    ['Who should decide how historical objects are presented?', 'Curators provide research expertise, but communities connected with the objects may contribute knowledge that formal records omit. Their views should be considered without replacing evidence with any single preferred story. Transparent labels can explain disagreement and uncertainty, helping visitors see how an interpretation was reached.'],
    ['Can interactive displays make museums less serious?', 'They can, if they distract from the objects or simplify difficult history into a game. But a well-designed activity can reveal how a tool worked or how evidence is interpreted. The test is whether interaction deepens understanding, rather than merely keeping visitors entertained for a few minutes.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Libraries as community learning spaces', [
    ['Why do libraries still matter when information is online?', 'Access to information is not the same as access to reliable help or a suitable place to study. A library can provide both, including computers for people without them. Printed collections remain important too, so the role has expanded rather than disappeared because searching the internet is possible.'],
    ['Should libraries host noisy group activities?', 'Some group activities serve a useful purpose, but they should not remove the quiet space that other users need. Separate rooms and predictable schedules can make both possible. A language conversation group, for instance, works better in a meeting room than among readers trying to concentrate.'],
    ['How can libraries support adults returning to education?', 'They can provide accessible study resources, guidance on finding reliable sources and flexible opening hours. Adults may be balancing work and family responsibilities, so evening support can be particularly valuable. Staff should help learners navigate resources rather than assume everyone remembers the study methods they used at school.'],
    ['Is lending equipment a suitable library service?', 'It can be, where there is demand and the equipment supports learning or participation. Lending a recording device may allow someone to complete a community-history project. The library must still budget for maintenance and explain safe use; buying equipment without a lending plan could create costly unused stock.'],
    ['How should a library decide which resources to buy?', 'Usage records are useful, but they should be combined with requests and an assessment of gaps. A resource used by fewer people may still be essential for a small language community. Decisions should balance broad demand with the library’s responsibility to offer reasonable access to underserved users.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Commercial influences on sport', [
    ['What benefits does sponsorship bring to sport?', 'It can pay for equipment, travel and facilities that a club could not afford from membership fees alone. A local team may gain opportunities to compete elsewhere. However, the sponsor’s expectations should be clear, because financial support can also influence which activities receive attention and how events are promoted.'],
    ['Can professional sport encourage ordinary people to exercise?', 'Successful athletes can make a sport visible and inspire interest, but watching a match does not automatically create a habit. Affordable facilities and beginner-friendly coaching are needed to turn enthusiasm into participation. Without those, a major sporting event may produce excitement while everyday activity remains unchanged.'],
    ['Should children focus on winning competitions?', 'Competition can teach effort and dealing with disappointment, but winning should not be the only measure of progress. Children develop at different rates, and an early emphasis on results can push some out. Skill improvement, cooperation and enjoyment provide reasons to continue even without frequent victories.'],
    ['How does television coverage affect sports that receive little attention?', 'Limited coverage can make sponsorship and recruitment harder, because people rarely see those sports being played. Yet a small sport may serve its community well without a huge audience. Public broadcasters can offer some variety, while clubs use local events to demonstrate what participation actually involves.'],
    ['Who should pay for maintaining community sports facilities?', 'Users can contribute where fees remain affordable, but facilities also provide wider health and social benefits. Public support is justified for accessible provision, especially in areas with few alternatives. Long-term maintenance should be included from the beginning, rather than funding a new building and leaving its upkeep uncertain.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Tourism in fragile environments', [
    ['Why do visitors choose places with vulnerable natural environments?', 'They often want scenery and experiences that feel different from everyday urban life. Seeing wildlife or walking in a remote landscape can be memorable. The difficulty is that the qualities attracting visitors may be damaged by heavy use, so popularity can undermine the experience people originally sought.'],
    ['How can visitor numbers be managed fairly?', 'Reservations or daily limits can protect a site, but access should not depend only on who can pay most. Allocating some affordable places and explaining the ecological reasons improves fairness. Nearby communities also need a voice, since they live with the effects even when visitor income benefits others.'],
    ['Does eco-tourism always protect nature?', 'No, the label can be used loosely. A tour may advertise conservation while disturbing wildlife or creating waste. Visitors need specific evidence, such as group-size limits and locally accountable management. Genuine protection also requires monitoring outcomes, rather than assuming that a nature-themed holiday is beneficial by definition.'],
    ['What responsibilities do tourists have?', 'They should follow local rules, stay on appropriate paths and avoid treating animals as props for photographs. Preparing before travel helps, because some impacts are not obvious to a visitor. Operators and authorities also have responsibilities; individual care cannot compensate for poorly designed access or inadequate waste facilities.'],
    ['Can virtual experiences reduce pressure on popular natural sites?', 'They may satisfy some curiosity and help people prepare for a visit, but they will not replace the appeal of being there. They are especially useful for people unable to travel. Protection still requires practical visitor management rather than expecting a digital alternative to eliminate physical demand.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Reliability in public transport', [
    ['Why is reliability important when people choose transport?', 'People usually plan journeys around obligations, not just the journey itself. A bus that arrives unpredictably can threaten a work shift or a medical appointment. Even a low fare may not compensate for that risk, so regular users value a service they can reasonably depend on.'],
    ['Should transport authorities focus on speed or frequency?', 'It depends on the route, but frequent services often reduce the overall inconvenience of waiting and missed connections. A very fast bus that runs only twice a day may be unsuitable for ordinary errands. Authorities should consider the entire journey, including access and waiting, rather than only vehicle speed.'],
    ['How can accurate travel information improve a service?', 'It helps passengers make decisions when delays occur, such as choosing another route or informing an employer. However, information must be trustworthy and available beyond smartphone apps. An incorrect arrival estimate can be worse than an honest explanation that the timing is uncertain because it encourages misplaced confidence.'],
    ['Are rural transport needs different from urban ones?', 'Rural routes often serve fewer people across larger distances, so copying a frequent city timetable may be unrealistic. Connections to essential services are still important. Smaller vehicles or planned community transport can help, but they need dependable booking arrangements rather than leaving residents unsure whether a journey is possible.'],
    ['What would persuade more drivers to use public transport?', 'A connected, predictable service matters more than a single attractive feature. Someone may try a bus if parking is expensive, but they will not continue if the return journey is unreliable. Safe stops, usable ticketing and suitable hours together make the alternative credible for everyday travel.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Local food and shopping habits', [
    ['Why do some consumers prefer locally produced food?', 'They may value freshness, familiarity with the producer or support for nearby businesses. Buying at a farm stall can make production feel less distant. But local origin alone does not guarantee quality or lower environmental impact; the method of production and the season also matter.'],
    ['What challenges do small food producers face?', 'They often lack the storage, transport and bargaining power available to large suppliers. A small grower may have an excellent crop but struggle to supply the same quantity every week. Shared distribution and realistic contracts can help without forcing every producer to operate like a major company.'],
    ['Can markets compete with supermarkets?', 'They can offer personal advice, unusual products and a distinctive atmosphere rather than matching every supermarket price. Convenience still matters, especially for working households. Clear opening times and reliable basic provision help markets retain customers who enjoy the experience but cannot make a separate trip for every ingredient.'],
    ['Should schools teach children where food comes from?', 'Yes, because it connects everyday eating with farming, labour and resources. A growing project can make those connections concrete, provided teachers explain that a small school garden is not representative of every farm. Practical activities work best alongside discussion of how food reaches shops and homes.'],
    ['Will online grocery shopping remove traditional markets?', 'It may reduce routine shopping trips, but markets also provide social interaction and sensory choices that delivery does not reproduce. Some can combine online ordering with collection. Survival depends on adapting useful services, rather than assuming customers will continue attending solely because markets have existed for a long time.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Flexible housing and changing households', [
    ['Why do housing needs change over a person’s life?', 'Work, relationships, health and caring responsibilities can all change how much space or access someone needs. A flat suitable for a single worker may become difficult with a child or reduced mobility. Housing policy should therefore allow movement and adaptation rather than assume one arrangement fits permanently.'],
    ['What makes a neighbourhood suitable for different generations?', 'It needs accessible routes, varied housing and services that support both independence and contact. A playground alone does not meet older residents’ needs, and a quiet residential area may lack places for teenagers. Shared spaces can work when designers recognise those differences instead of serving only an imagined average resident.'],
    ['Is owning a home always better than renting?', 'Ownership can provide stability and control, but it also brings maintenance costs and makes moving less flexible. Renting may suit someone whose job location changes. The quality of protections matters greatly: a secure, well-managed rental can be preferable to ownership that leaves a household financially overstretched.'],
    ['How can homes be adapted without major rebuilding?', 'Simple changes such as clearer lighting, removable partitions or an accessible entrance can improve use, depending on the property. Planning adaptability from the start is easier than making expensive changes later. Not every building can meet every need, though, so advice should be specific rather than promise a universal solution.'],
    ['Should cities provide more small homes?', 'Smaller households often need them, but floor area should not be reduced until basic comfort disappears. Good storage, light and access to shared space can make a compact home workable. A varied supply is more useful than assuming that either large family houses or tiny units will meet everyone’s needs.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Childcare and working patterns', [
    ['Why can childcare arrangements influence career decisions?', 'Care has to fit actual working hours, not just a standard daytime schedule. A parent offered an evening shift may be unable to accept it even if the pay is attractive. Availability, reliability and travel time all matter, so childcare can shape employment choices as much as professional interests do.'],
    ['What makes a childcare service trustworthy?', 'Clear safeguarding procedures, trained staff and honest communication with families are essential. Parents need to know who is responsible and what happens if a child becomes unwell. A colourful building may look welcoming, but dependable routines and appropriate supervision provide a stronger basis for trust than decoration.'],
    ['Should employers help employees with childcare?', 'They can help through predictable schedules and reasonable flexibility, even if running a nursery is impractical. A last-minute shift change may create more difficulty than a modest pay difference. Support should also be fair to other carers, such as employees looking after elderly relatives, rather than recognise only one family situation.'],
    ['How do grandparents’ caring roles affect families?', 'Their help can provide familiar care and allow parents to work, but it should not be treated as unlimited. Grandparents may have health needs or plans of their own. Families benefit when they agree practical boundaries and alternatives instead of assuming affection creates a permanent obligation to provide care.'],
    ['Can public childcare funding improve equality?', 'It can reduce a major barrier to employment, particularly for households unable to pay commercial fees. However, funding is less useful if no places exist locally or the hours are unsuitable. Improving staff supply and access needs to accompany financial support if the policy is to change real opportunities.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Skills training and economic opportunity', [
    ['Why does acquiring a skill not always lead to a better job?', 'The skill must match opportunities that actually exist, and employers need a way to recognise it. A person may complete a useful course but live far from relevant vacancies. Transport, caring responsibilities and recruitment practices can still prevent progress, so training is only one part of economic opportunity.'],
    ['What kinds of support make adult training accessible?', 'Flexible times, clear entry requirements and help with practical costs make a difference. Someone working irregular shifts may need modular study rather than a long fixed programme. Good advice also matters, because an adult investing scarce time should understand what the course can realistically lead to.'],
    ['Should training providers promise employment?', 'They should describe evidence about previous outcomes, but a guaranteed job can be misleading unless an employer has genuinely committed to it. Economic conditions change and learners have different circumstances. Transparent information about qualifications, placements and recruitment links is more useful than an attractive promise with hidden conditions.'],
    ['How can practical experience complement classroom learning?', 'It exposes learners to decisions and constraints that exercises may simplify. An electrical trainee can understand a diagram but still need supervised practice identifying a fault safely. Experience should be structured and supported, though; unpaid work without teaching can become cheap labour rather than meaningful training.'],
    ['Who benefits when workers gain transferable skills?', 'Workers gain options, and employers can adapt more easily when tasks change. Communities may benefit if those skills help new businesses survive. The gains are not evenly distributed, so employers should give staff time to learn instead of expecting all development to happen privately after work.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Household renewable energy', [
    ['Why are some households interested in producing their own electricity?', 'They may want lower running costs, more predictable bills or a smaller environmental impact. Seeing the equipment on the roof also makes energy production more tangible. The decision still depends on upfront expense and suitability, so interest alone does not mean a household can afford or install a system.'],
    ['What should people consider before buying rooftop solar panels?', 'They should check the roof, expected generation, installation quality and how much power they use during daylight. A neighbour’s savings may not apply to a differently positioned home. Clear estimates and warranty terms are important, particularly when sales material emphasises a best-case return rather than a realistic range.'],
    ['Can renters benefit from household renewable technology?', 'They may benefit if landlords install it and share the savings fairly, but tenants usually cannot make major building changes themselves. Shared local schemes offer another possibility. Policies should include renters rather than direct every subsidy towards homeowners who already have the money and authority to invest.'],
    ['Should public funds subsidise private energy installations?', 'Some subsidy is reasonable when it supports broader environmental goals and helps adoption, but its distribution matters. Giving large discounts mainly to wealthy households may widen inequality. Support for suitable low-income homes or shared systems can spread benefits more fairly while still encouraging cleaner energy production.'],
    ['Does generating electricity remove the need to save energy?', 'No, because generation varies and equipment still uses resources. Insulation and efficient appliances can reduce demand whether or not a home has panels. Combining lower consumption with cleaner supply is usually more sensible than treating rooftop equipment as permission to waste power whenever it is available.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Ethics in scientific research', [
    ['Why does scientific research need ethical rules?', 'Research can affect people who have less power than the investigators, or who do not fully understand the risks. Rules help protect consent, privacy and safety. They also support public trust, but they need meaningful enforcement; a completed form alone does not make a poorly explained study ethical.'],
    ['How should researchers explain a complicated study to participants?', 'They should use clear language, give people time to ask questions and separate necessary information from technical detail. Someone should understand what participation involves and whether they can withdraw. Checking comprehension is better than assuming a long document has informed everyone simply because they signed it.'],
    ['Can commercial funding affect research decisions?', 'It can create pressure to focus on profitable questions or present favourable findings. That does not mean every funded study is unreliable, but conflicts should be disclosed. Independent review and publication of negative results help readers assess the evidence rather than accept or reject it solely because of the sponsor.'],
    ['Should uncertain scientific findings be reported to the public?', 'Yes, if the uncertainty is explained honestly and the findings are relevant. Waiting for absolute certainty could delay useful information, but a preliminary result should not become a sensational promise. Reports can distinguish an early association from a demonstrated cause and explain what further work is needed.'],
    ['How can communities contribute to research priorities?', 'People affected by an issue can identify practical questions that researchers overlook. Patients may value relief from a daily symptom more than an impressive laboratory measure. Their input should complement technical expertise, helping projects address real needs while preserving methods capable of producing reliable answers.'],
  ]),
  discussion('New residents and local services', [
    ['Why do people moving to a town sometimes struggle to use its services?', 'Local procedures may be familiar to long-term residents but unexplained to newcomers. Finding a doctor or understanding waste collection can involve several separate offices. Language differences make this harder, but even someone fluent may need a clear starting point and reliable information about which organisation handles each task.'],
    ['What information should a welcome guide prioritise?', 'It should cover essential steps such as healthcare registration, transport, schools and emergency contacts, with clear links to current information. A long history of the town is interesting but less urgent. The guide should also explain where to ask questions, because not every household will fit the standard examples.'],
    ['Can community events help newcomers settle?', 'They can create informal contacts and make it easier to ask ordinary questions without arranging an official appointment. However, an event helps less if it is expensive, inaccessible or advertised only through existing social groups. Organisers should consider people who have not yet learned where local information appears.'],
    ['Should services provide information in several languages?', 'For essential matters, selected translations can prevent serious misunderstandings and save staff time. They should reflect local needs rather than attempt every language indiscriminately. Plain language in the main version is still important, since complicated writing can exclude residents regardless of which language they speak at home.'],
    ['How can towns encourage contact between newcomers and established residents?', 'Shared activities with a practical purpose often work better than asking people simply to socialise. A neighbourhood garden or school project creates reasons to cooperate. It is important to avoid expecting newcomers to do all the adapting; established groups also need to welcome different schedules, skills and experiences.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Shared offices and flexible workspaces', [
    ['Why do independent workers use shared offices?', 'They may want reliable facilities and social contact without renting a private office. Working near others can provide structure and occasional professional connections. However, not everyone needs collaboration every day, so a good space should allow quiet independent work as well as opportunities to meet people.'],
    ['What disadvantages can a shared workspace have?', 'Noise, privacy concerns and inconsistent availability can make some tasks difficult. A consultant discussing confidential information cannot always use an open desk. Flexible membership is useful, but users need clear rules about meeting rooms and phone calls if the space is to support serious work rather than constant interruption.'],
    ['Does office design affect cooperation?', 'It can make contact easier, but an open room does not automatically create trust or useful teamwork. People need clear roles and time to concentrate. Shared kitchens or planned discussions can encourage exchange without removing every private area, which may help cooperation more than forcing everyone into constant visibility.'],
    ['Should employers let staff choose where they work?', 'Choice can improve focus and reduce travel, but it needs to fit the task and team obligations. A designer may work well remotely yet still benefit from an in-person review. Agreed availability and outcome expectations are more practical than treating either full freedom or constant attendance as universally best.'],
    ['How might shared offices influence local neighbourhoods?', 'They can bring daytime customers to cafés and allow people to work near home. If rents rise sharply, though, existing community uses may be displaced. The effect depends on the building and local demand, so councils should assess specific benefits rather than assume every new workspace revitalises an area.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Automation in healthcare', [
    ['Which healthcare tasks are suitable for automation?', 'Routine administration and some pattern checking can benefit, particularly when staff otherwise repeat the same steps. An automated reminder may reduce missed appointments. Clinical decisions require stronger oversight, because unusual cases and personal circumstances matter; a system should support professional judgement rather than hide it behind an automatic result.'],
    ['Why might patients hesitate to trust a medical system?', 'They may not understand how it reached a recommendation or who is responsible if something goes wrong. A person also wants their individual concerns heard. Explaining the system’s role and allowing discussion with a clinician can build trust more effectively than simply stating that the technology is advanced.'],
    ['Can automation give healthcare workers more time with patients?', 'It can if it removes genuinely repetitive work and is integrated properly. Poorly designed software may instead create extra checking and data entry. Hospitals should measure the effect on staff time and patient experience rather than assume that installing a system automatically frees everyone from administrative duties.'],
    ['What risks arise when medical data are used by new technology?', 'Sensitive information could be accessed improperly or used beyond the purpose patients understood. Inaccurate records can also affect recommendations. Strong access controls and clear correction procedures are necessary, along with limits on commercial reuse; privacy should not depend only on a lengthy consent statement few people can understand.'],
    ['Should hospitals introduce new technology quickly?', 'They should respond to promising evidence, but controlled trials and staff preparation are important. A staged introduction can reveal problems before they affect every patient. Urgency varies by the benefit and risk, so cautious evaluation should not mean rejecting useful tools indefinitely, nor should enthusiasm bypass necessary checks.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Remote education and classroom interaction', [
    ['What makes an online lesson engaging?', 'Learners need a clear task and opportunities to respond, rather than only a long video to watch. A teacher might ask them to compare examples and explain a choice. The technology should support that exchange, but too many features can distract from the material instead of making it clearer.'],
    ['Which learners may find remote study particularly useful?', 'People who cannot travel regularly because of work, disability or caring responsibilities may gain access to courses they would otherwise miss. They still need suitable equipment and study time. Flexibility is valuable, but describing remote study as convenient can overlook the practical barriers faced at home.'],
    ['Why is practical training difficult to provide entirely online?', 'Some skills require physical feedback and supervised use of equipment. Watching a demonstration does not show whether a trainee can perform safely. Online preparation can make practical sessions more efficient, but it usually cannot replace direct observation where errors could harm the learner or other people.'],
    ['How can teachers judge participation in remote classes?', 'They can use brief responses, discussion and small pieces of work to see how learners are thinking. Keeping a camera on is not reliable evidence of understanding and may be unsuitable for privacy reasons. Several forms of participation give a fairer picture than judging students mainly by their visible screen presence.'],
    ['Will classroom learning become unnecessary?', 'I doubt it, because classrooms provide social interaction, practical support and a shared routine that many learners value. Remote options can expand access and make some content easier to revisit. The likely benefit comes from combining formats appropriately, rather than declaring one setting sufficient for every subject and learner.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Historical memory and public monuments', [
    ['Why do societies preserve monuments?', 'They mark events or people considered important and make history visible in everyday places. A monument can also express the values of the time when it was built. That means it is useful evidence, but not a complete or neutral account of the past by itself.'],
    ['What should happen when a monument becomes controversial?', 'The first step should be explaining the history and hearing from affected communities, rather than assuming there is only one possible response. Added interpretation, relocation or removal may each be appropriate in different circumstances. Keeping a record matters even when a society decides that public honour is no longer justified.'],
    ['How do family memories differ from official history?', 'Family stories preserve personal experiences that formal records may miss, but they can also be selective or change through retelling. Official accounts have their own limitations. Comparing both sources helps people understand how a large event affected ordinary lives without treating either as automatically complete.'],
    ['Should schools teach conflicting interpretations of history?', 'Yes, when pupils have enough context to understand the evidence behind them. Disagreement can show how historians work rather than make the subject seem arbitrary. Teachers should distinguish a supported interpretation from a claim without evidence, so open discussion does not imply that every version is equally reliable.'],
    ['Can digital archives change the way people investigate the past?', 'They make records available to people who cannot visit distant collections and allow different sources to be compared quickly. Search tools can still miss material or remove context. Users need to understand the collection’s coverage and original source, rather than assume that what appears first in a search is the whole story.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Public art and community decisions', [
    ['What can public art contribute to a neighbourhood?', 'It can create a recognisable place, encourage curiosity and reflect experiences that residents value. A mural made with local participation may feel different from an imported sculpture. Its contribution depends on the setting and maintenance, though; prominent size alone does not ensure lasting meaning or public benefit.'],
    ['Who should choose art displayed in shared spaces?', 'Artists and specialists can provide expertise, but residents should have meaningful input because they use the space daily. Complete agreement is unlikely. A transparent process explaining the purpose, cost and alternatives can make disagreement manageable without turning every artwork into a competition for the safest possible choice.'],
    ['Should all public artworks be permanent?', 'No, temporary work can allow experimentation and make room for changing communities. Permanent pieces need a long-term care plan, especially if their materials deteriorate. The choice should fit the purpose; an event-based installation may be valuable without becoming a permanent maintenance obligation for the council.'],
    ['How important is explanation beside an artwork?', 'A concise explanation can reveal connections that viewers would otherwise miss, particularly when the work refers to local history. It should not tell people exactly what they must feel. Providing context while leaving room for interpretation is more helpful than either a dense academic text or no information at all.'],
    ['Can public art justify spending when other services need money?', 'Sometimes, but the case should be proportionate and clear. Small projects can support community identity without consuming a large budget, whereas an expensive commission deserves closer scrutiny. Cultural value matters, yet it does not remove the need to compare costs and preserve essential services.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Music across generations and cultures', [
    ['Why do musical preferences differ between generations?', 'People associate music with the places, friendships and technology of their youth. Repeated exposure also makes certain sounds familiar. Preferences are not fixed by age, though; a shared performance or recommendation can introduce someone to a style they had previously dismissed without really hearing it.'],
    ['How does music travel between cultures?', 'Migration, recordings and collaborative performances allow styles and instruments to meet. Musicians may adapt an influence to express their own circumstances rather than copy it exactly. Respectful exchange should acknowledge its sources, especially when commercial success benefits people far removed from the community that developed the tradition.'],
    ['Should traditional music be preserved exactly as it was?', 'Recordings and careful teaching can preserve important knowledge, but living traditions also change through performance. Insisting on one frozen version may discourage younger musicians. It is useful to distinguish documentation of an earlier form from creative development, so both preservation and new work can have a place.'],
    ['What does live performance offer beyond recorded music?', 'The shared atmosphere and unpredictable details make it a different experience. Performers respond to the audience, and listeners may notice instruments more clearly in person. Recordings offer convenience and access, so one is not simply better; live events provide a kind of participation that listening alone does not reproduce.'],
    ['Can music education help people understand other cultures?', 'It can introduce histories and practices that might otherwise feel unfamiliar, especially if teaching explains the context rather than only the tune. Learners should hear from knowledgeable practitioners where possible. Playing one song does not create complete cultural understanding, but it can open a useful line of curiosity.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Recipes and cultural identity', [
    ['Why do family recipes have emotional significance?', 'They connect food with people and occasions, such as a grandparent preparing a dish during a holiday. The memory may matter more than perfect ingredients. Recipes also change between households, so their value often lies in the relationship and practice rather than one supposedly correct written version.'],
    ['What happens when traditional dishes are adapted for new places?', 'Available ingredients and local tastes may lead to changes, while the dish still preserves a connection with its origin. A family living abroad might substitute a vegetable they can find easily. Adaptation is not automatically a loss, but it helps to explain what has changed rather than present it as identical.'],
    ['Should schools teach practical cooking?', 'Basic cooking helps pupils understand food, manage a budget and become more independent. Lessons should include safe preparation and affordable choices, not only attractive finished dishes. Access to equipment at home varies, so tasks should not assume every child can reproduce a complicated recipe outside school.'],
    ['Can social media help preserve cooking traditions?', 'It can record techniques that are difficult to describe in writing and connect people with knowledgeable cooks. Short videos may leave out important context or turn a family practice into a simplified trend. Longer explanations and acknowledgement of sources make the material more useful for genuine learning.'],
    ['Why might convenience food remain popular despite interest in cooking?', 'Time and energy are real constraints, particularly after a long workday or with caring responsibilities. Interest does not guarantee the opportunity to prepare everything from scratch. Convenient options vary in quality, so the discussion should focus on practical choices rather than judge every household by an idealised cooking routine.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Sleep and modern working hours', [
    ['Why can modern work make regular sleep difficult?', 'Some jobs involve shifts, late messages or long journeys, all of which disturb a predictable routine. A worker may be physically home but still checking urgent requests. Personal habits matter, but advice about sleep is less effective when the work schedule itself leaves too little consistent time to rest.'],
    ['What responsibilities do employers have for staff fatigue?', 'They should plan reasonable shifts and avoid creating a culture where constant availability is rewarded. Fatigue can affect safety as well as wellbeing, particularly in driving or healthcare. Employees also have responsibilities, but an employer cannot ignore a damaging schedule and treat every resulting problem as a private lifestyle choice.'],
    ['Can flexible hours improve rest?', 'They can help someone match work to commuting or family demands, but flexibility needs limits. If it means being available at any hour, rest may become less predictable. Clear expectations about contact and total workload are necessary for flexible arrangements to provide control rather than extend the working day.'],
    ['Why do people sometimes sacrifice sleep for leisure?', 'After work and domestic duties, late evening may feel like the only time they control. Staying awake can seem like reclaiming that time, even when it makes the next day harder. Recognising that pressure is useful, because simply telling people to go to bed earlier ignores why they resist.'],
    ['Should schools start later in the morning?', 'A later start may suit adolescent sleep patterns, but transport and family schedules need consideration. The benefit could be reduced if homework and activities simply move later too. A sensible trial should examine attendance, alertness and practical effects rather than assume that changing one clock time solves every problem.'],
  ]),
  discussion('Public consultation and local decisions', [
    ['Why do authorities consult residents before making changes?', 'Residents can identify daily problems that planning documents overlook, such as a crossing used by children or delivery access for a small shop. Consultation also explains trade-offs. It should influence decisions meaningfully, though; asking for views after every important choice has been made can damage trust rather than build it.'],
    ['How can consultation include people who rarely attend meetings?', 'Authorities can use several channels, including short surveys, accessible sessions and outreach through trusted local groups. A weekday evening meeting will not suit everyone. They should also explain proposals clearly, because people may avoid commenting when the material is technical or they are unsure whether their experience counts as useful evidence.'],
    ['Does the most popular option always deserve to be chosen?', 'No, popularity is important but must be weighed against safety, rights and effects on smaller groups. A car-parking proposal may attract many votes while making a pavement inaccessible. Decision-makers should explain that reasoning openly, rather than either ignore the majority or treat consultation as a simple vote on every issue.'],
    ['What information helps residents compare local proposals?', 'They need realistic costs, likely effects and a description of who benefits or faces inconvenience. Maps and practical examples can make alternatives easier to understand. Presenting only the preferred option’s advantages prevents informed comparison and may create avoidable disagreement when its disadvantages become visible later.'],
    ['How should authorities respond after consultation ends?', 'They should publish the main findings and explain which changes were made, along with reasons for rejecting other suggestions. People do not need every request accepted, but they need evidence that it was considered. A clear response helps residents decide whether participating in future consultations is worthwhile.'],
  ]),
];

